.El
.Pp
A command that would replace or abandon a modified buffer \(em
.Ic edit ,
.Ic next ,
.Ic prev ,
.Ic rewind ,
.Ic recover ,
.Ic quit
\(em fails while it has unwritten changes, unless given
.Ql \&!
.Pq for example Ic recover! .
.Pp
The commands of a
.Ic global
or
.Ic v
follow the pattern; a line ending in a backslash continues the list on the
next line, each line a command.
After
.Ic append ,
.Ic insert
or
.Ic change
in the list, the lines are text, up to a line holding only
.Ql \&.
or the end of the list.
The list may not contain
.Ic global ,
.Ic v
or
.Ic undo .
.Pp
Addresses, ranges, marks, search addresses, relative offsets, and
delimiter-aware command chaining with
.Ql |
are supported.
An address left out beside
.Ql \&,
or
.Ql \&;
is the current line; of more addresses than a command takes, the last are
used;
.Ql \&;
makes the address before it the current line.
A range whose first line is after its second is an error, and so is line 0
for any command but
.Ic append ,
.Ic insert ,
.Ic read ,
.Ic put
and
.Ic = .
.Pp
.Ic delete ,
.Ic yank ,
.Ic print ,
.Ic list ,
.Ic number
and
.Ic join
take a count, which makes the range that many lines from its last, and
.Ic delete
and
.Ic join
the flags
.Ql p ,
.Ql #
and
.Ql l ,
which print the current line after.
.Ic delete ,
.Ic yank
and
.Ic put
take a buffer name,
.Ql a
to
.Ql z ;
.Ql A
to
.Ql Z
name the same buffers, appended to.
Each command leaves the current line as POSIX describes \(em
.Ic delete
the line after those deleted,
.Ic copy ,
.Ic move ,
.Ic put
and
.Ic read
the last line they placed,
.Ic join
the joined line \(em and editing a file makes its last line current.
.Ic join
drops the leading blanks of each line it appends and puts two spaces after
a line ending in
.Ql \&. ,
none before a
.Ql \&)
or after a blank, and one otherwise;
.Ic join!
joins the lines as they are.
The editor accepts the traditional
.Ql \&"
command-tail comment form outside delimiter bodies.
.Pp
A pattern \(em in a search address,
.Ic global ,
.Ic v
or
.Ic substitute
\(em is a POSIX basic regular expression
.Pq Xr regex 3 ,
with
.Ql \e<
and
.Ql \e>
matching at the start and end of a word, and
.Ql ~
standing for the replacement text of the last
.Ic substitute .
A backslash before the delimiter makes the delimiter part of the pattern.
An empty pattern is the last pattern any of these commands used, and a
pattern that will not compile is reported as an error.
With the
.Ic magic
option off,
.Ql \&. ,
.Ql \&[ ,
.Ql *
and
.Ql ~
are ordinary characters and their escaped forms are the special ones.
.Pp
In the replacement of
.Ic substitute ,
.Ql &
stands for the matched text,
.Ql \e1
to
.Ql \e9
for its subexpressions and
.Ql ~
for the last replacement;
.Ql \eu
and
.Ql \el
make the next character upper or lower case, and
.Ql \eU
and
.Ql \eL
the characters up to
.Ql \eE
or
.Ql \ee .
A backslash before any other character stands for that character, and
before a newline splits the line there.
With
.Ic nomagic ,
.Ql \e&
and
.Ql \e~
are the special forms.
.Pp
After the replacement
.Ic substitute
takes options, a count and flags:
.Ql g
replaces every match on a line,
.Ql c
asks about each one (answer
.Ql y
to replace it,
.Ql q
to stop),
.Ql r
in a repeat uses the last pattern rather than the last substitute's, and
.Ql &
keeps the previous substitute's options; a count makes the range that many
lines from its last; and
.Ql p ,
.Ql #
and
.Ql l
print the last line changed.
.Ic &
and
.Ic s
without a pattern repeat the previous substitute with the options given
.Pq so Ic && keeps its options .
.Pp
A command line that ends in a backslash continues on the next line, the
backslash and the newline part of the command.
.Pp
.Ic print
and
.Ic list
show a control character as
.Ql ^ Ns Ar X
and a byte that is no printable character \(em a byte in no valid UTF-8
sequence, or one of a C1 control's \(em as
.Ql \e Ns Ar ooo ,
its octal value, so that no line can send the terminal a byte it would
act on.
.Pp
.Ic \&! Ns Ar command
runs
.Ar command
by the
.Ic shell
option's program;
.Ar addr1 , Ns Ar addr2 Ns Ic \&! Ns Ar command
replaces those lines with what the command writes when they are its
input.
In the commands of
.Ic \&! ,
.Ic r \&!
and
.Ic w \&! ,
.Ql %
stands for the current file name,
.Ql #
for the alternate file name and
.Ql \&!
for the last such command; a backslash before one of them makes it the
character.
When one was expanded, the command is written before it runs, but with
.Fl s .
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl R
Start in readonly mode.
Plain writes are rejected unless forced with
.Ql \&! .
.It Fl r
Recover the named file from a preserved recovery image.
If no recovery image exists,
.Nm
fails with an error.
With no file,
.Nm
lists the files that can be recovered and exits.
.It Fl w Ar size
Set the
.Ic window
option to
.Ar size .
.It Fl
The same as
.Fl s
(obsolescent).
.It Fl s
Batch mode: no prompts, file messages or other interactive output; the
first command read that fails ends the session with a failing status (see
.Sx EXIT STATUS ) ;
and no startup commands are read from
.Ev EXINIT ,
.Pa ~/.exrc
or
.Pa ./.exrc .
Commands given with
.Fl c
still run.
.It Fl S
Start in secure mode.
Shell escapes and shell-capable commands are disabled.
.It Fl v
Start in visual mode, as
.Xr vi 1
does.
.Ic visual
enters it later, on the same buffer \(em modified or not \(em with its
undo history, marks and named buffers;
.Ic Q
in visual mode returns to
.Nm .
.It Fl c Ar command
Execute
.Ar command
after startup processing.
This option may be specified more than once.
.It Fl t Ar tag
Start by editing the file and location named by
.Ar tag .
.It Ar +command
Execute
.Ar command
after startup processing.
Common forms include
.Ql +42
to move to line 42 and
.Ql +next
to advance within the argument list.
.It Ar file
Open
.Ar file
as the current buffer.
If multiple files are given, they become the initial argument list.
.El
.Sh SET OPTIONS
.Ic set
with no argument lists the options whose values differ from their
defaults,
.Ic set all
every option and its value, and
.Ic set Ar opt Ns \&?
(or
.Ic set no Ns Ar opt Ns \&? )
the value of one.
Every option POSIX lists is accepted under its name and abbreviation, and
reported:
autoindent (ai), autoprint (ap), autowrite (aw), beautify (bf), directory
(dir), edcompatible (ed), errorbells (eb), exrc (ex), ignorecase (ic), lisp,
list, magic, mesg, number (nu), paragraphs (para), prompt, readonly (ro),
redraw, remap, report, scroll (scr), sections (sect), shell (sh), shiftwidth
(sw), showmatch (sm), showmode (smd), slowopen (slow), tabstop (ts),
taglength (tl), tags, term, terse, warn, window (wi), wrapmargin (wm),
wrapscan (ws) and writeany (wa).
Those with an effect are:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Ic number , nu
Number printed output in
.Nm .
.It Ic list , li
Render tabs and line endings in list form.
.It Ic autoindent , ai
Shared option state used by the visual frontend's indentation helpers.
.It Ic ignorecase , ic
Make searches,
.Ic global ,
.Ic v ,
and
.Ic substitute
case-insensitive.
.It Ic readonly , ro
Mark the session readonly.
.It Ic showmode , smd
Show vi's insert and replace mode banners.
Off by default.
.It Ic wrapscan , ws
Wrap searches at buffer boundaries.
.It Ic magic
Give
.Ql \&. ,
.Ql \&[ ,
.Ql *
and
.Ql ~
their special meaning in patterns.
It is on by default.
.It Ic writeany , wa
Let
.Ic write
replace an existing file other than the current one without
.Ql \&! .
It is off by default.
.It Ic exrc , ex
Read
.Pa ./.exrc
at startup.
It is off by default and has effect only when set by
.Ev EXINIT
or
.Pa ~/.exrc .
.It Ic tabstop= , ts=
Set tab rendering width.
The default is 8.
.It Ic scroll= , sc=
Set the visual frontend's half-page scroll amount once explicitly configured.
.It Ic tags=
Set the comma-separated tag-file search path used by
.Ic tag
and related visual commands.
.It Ic directory= , dir=
The directory under which the user's recovery directory,
.Pa exvi- Ns Ar uid ,
is kept.
The default is
.Pa /var/tmp .
.It Ic shell= , sh=
The program that runs the commands of
.Ic \&! ,
.Ic r \&!
and
.Ic w \&! ,
as
.Ar shell Fl c Ar command .
The default is
.Ev SHELL ,
or
.Pa /bin/sh
when that is unset.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Ev EXINIT
If set, supplies startup commands, and
.Pa ~/.exrc
is not loaded.
Ignored with
.Fl s .
.It Ev SHELL
The default of the
.Ic shell
option.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Pa ~/.exrc
Per-user startup commands, loaded when
.Ev EXINIT
is not set, and only when ownership and directory safety checks pass.
.It Pa ./.exrc
Per-directory startup commands, loaded after
.Ev EXINIT
or
.Pa ~/.exrc
only if one of them set the
.Ic exrc
option, only when the ownership and directory safety checks pass, and not
when it is
.Pa ~/.exrc
itself.
Neither
.Pa .exrc
is loaded with
.Fl s
or
.Fl S .
.It Pa tags
Default tag file name when the
.Ic tags
option is left at its default value.
Each line is a tag name, a file name and an address, separated by tabs.
The address is a line number or a
.Li / Ns Ar pattern Ns Li /
or
.Li \&? Ns Ar pattern Ns Li \&?
search, and nothing else: any other address is reported as a bad tag
address, never run as a command.
.It Pa /var/tmp/exvi- Ns Ar uid
The user's recovery directory, under the
.Ic directory
option's value: preserved recovery images, one per file, written by
.Ic preserve
and for sessions ended by
.Dv SIGHUP
or
.Dv SIGTERM .
It is created with mode 0700 and used only while it is the user's, not a
symbolic link and closed to everyone else; each image is created afresh
with mode 0600, and only an image the user owns is recovered.
Writing the file removes an image only if this session wrote or recovered
from it.
.El
.Sh ASYNCHRONOUS EVENTS
.Dv SIGINT
stops the current command (a
.Ic global
between the commands it runs) and ends text input;
.Nm
writes
.Ql Interrupt
and returns to the command prompt with the buffer as the command left it.
.Dv SIGHUP
and
.Dv SIGTERM
preserve a modified buffer and end the session.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The
.Nm
utility exits 0 on success and with a value greater than 0 if an error occurs.
When standard input is not a terminal, the first command read from it that
fails ends the session at once, with nothing more written, and with a
value greater than 0; so does the end of input while the buffer is
modified, as
.Ic quit
would refuse.
Commands given with
.Fl c
or
.Ar +command
do not end the session.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ex file.txt
ex +42 file.txt
ex -c 'set number' -c '%p' file.txt
ex -R file.txt
ex -r file.txt
ex -t main tags-demo.c
.Ed
.Sh NOTES
The supported Substrate contract is intentionally narrower than the union of
historical BSD,
nvi,
and Vim command sets.
Only the command names, abbreviations, and
.Ic :set
spellings documented here and in
.Pa docs/specs/exvi.md
are part of the supported interface.
.Pp
Readonly entry points such as
view
and restricted entry points such as
.Ql rex
are implemented through the shared frontend parser even though they are not
separate binaries today.
.Pp
A file whose last line has no newline is edited as if it had one, and is
written back with it.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr vi 1 ,
.Xr regex 3
